course: money
lesson: 3
title: Money around the world: euros, hryvnias, yuan and more
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every country chooses its own money, called its currency: Ukraine has the hryvnia (₴), China the yuan (¥), Japan the yen (¥), Nigeria the naira, the USA the dollar and the UK the pound, while many European countries share the euro (€).
explain: A 100-hryvnia note from Gran in Kyiv buys crisps in Kyiv, but a London shop will not take it, because London shops take pounds; to spend it you first swap it at a bank or exchange shop.
explain: The exchange rate says how much of one money you get for another; a big number on a price does not mean a big price, because a Japanese sweet may cost 100 yen and still be cheap.
explain: Money-changing shops give you a little less than the real rate and keep the difference as their pay, and airport shops often give the worst rates because travellers are in a hurry.
fact: A currency is the kind of money a country uses.
fact: Ukraine uses the hryvnia (₴), China the yuan (also called renminbi), Japan the yen, Nigeria the naira and Canada the Canadian dollar.
fact: About twenty European countries, including Ireland, France and Spain, share one currency, the euro.
fact: The exchange rate tells you how much of one currency you get for another, and it changes from day to day.
fact: Exchange shops give you less than the real rate and keep the difference; this is how they are paid.
fact: A small number on a foreign price tag does not mean it is cheap; you must think what it is in your own money.
fact: Canada's $1 coin is called a "loonie" because it shows a bird called a loon; the $2 coin is the "toonie".
fact: Each country's central bank looks after its money, which lets the country make its own money choices.
fact: Leftover foreign coins still have value abroad; many airports have charity boxes for them, and banks often will not swap coins back.
fact: When you buy online from another country, the price is changed into your money, and the bank may add a small fee.
fact: At Chinese New Year people give money in red envelopes for luck.
fact: Prices are different in every country and every year, so comparing them needs to know where and when.
exercise: What is a currency? | The kind of money a country uses | A coin made of real gold or silver | A shop that sells sweets from abroad | The price of a holiday in another country
exercise: Which currency does Ukraine use? | The hryvnia | The Chinese yuan | The Nigerian naira | The euro, like France
exercise: Olena's friend in London wants to buy crisps with a 100-hryvnia note. What will the London shop say? | It only takes pounds, so swap the note at a bank first | It takes any note with numbers on it | It will take it, but only for big shopping | The note is fake because it is not in pounds
exercise: If £1 is about 9 yuan, what is 50 yuan worth? | A bit more than £5 | About £50 in pounds | About 50p and no more | About £45 after the fee
exercise: Ben sees ice cream for "3" in Spain. What does it really cost? | 3 euros, which is about £2.50 | 3 pence, which is very cheap | 3 pounds, the same as at home | 30 euros, because prices are in tens
exercise: The exchange shop gives 1.10 euros for each pound. How many euros does Chloe's mum get for £100? | 110 euros | 120 euros | 100 euros | 90 euros
exercise: Why do exchange shops give a bit less than the real rate? | They keep the difference as their pay | The euro gets smaller on the plane journey | It is the law that you lose money abroad | Pounds are worth nothing outside Britain
exercise: In Canada, how many loonies make one toonie? | 2 | 4 | 10 | 5
exercise: Why does the UK not just use the same money as the whole world? | Its own money lets the country make its own money choices | Other countries refuse to let Britain use their money | One world money was tried and is against the law | Coins from other countries do not fit in British machines
exercise: Hannah has three €1 coins left after France. What can she do? | Keep them for next time or give them to an airport charity box | Spend them in a British shop as pounds | Throw them away, they are worth nothing | Swap them at any British shop for £3
exercise: Lucy's game says $4.99. It comes to about £4, plus a 20p bank fee. What does she pay? | £4.20 | £4.99 | £4.00 | £5.20
exercise: If £1 is about 190 yen, roughly what is 1,000 yen in pounds? | About £5 | About £1 | About £50 | About £190
exercise: What did Sami's dad mean by "In my day a meat pie was 5 naira. Now? Forget it"? | Prices in Nigeria have gone up a lot since he was young | Meat pies are no longer sold in Lagos | A naira is now worth more than before | Naira coins have become pies
exercise: Which group of countries shares one currency? | Many European countries using the euro | Every country in Asia using the yen | All English-speaking countries using the pound | Canada and Ukraine using the dollar
exercise: Max's cousin has three toonies. How many dollars is that? | $6 | $3 | $9 | $1.50
exercise: Why do airport money shops often give the worst rates? | Travellers are in a hurry and have no time to shop around | Planes need extra fuel paid for by the rate | Money is heavier at airports | The government sets airport rates lower
test: Your mate's like "a yen is basically a quid, innit?" What's the truth? | No, a pound is worth around 190 yen, so a yen is tiny | Yes, they're worth exactly the same | No, one yen is worth about £190 | Yes, but only in London
test: Bro on holiday in Japan: "this sweet costs 100, that's a rip-off!" ("Rip-off" means far too dear.) Is it? | No, 100 yen is only about 50p | Yes, 100 is a huge price for a sweet | Yes, 100 yen is about £100 | No, sweets are free in Japan
test: Canadian cousin says "grab a double-double, it's two toonies, eh" ("double-double" is a coffee with two creams and two sugars). How much? | $4 | $2 | $1 | $8
test: Dad: "Board says we buy at 1.20, we sell at 1.10." He swaps £50 into euros at 1.10. How many euros? | 55 euros | 60 euros | 50 euros | 65 euros
test: In the same shop, how many more euros would £100 have got at 1.20 than at 1.10? | 10 euros | 1 euro | 20 euros | 0 euros
test: What would happen if Hannah tried to pay a London newsagent with euro coins? | The shop would probably refuse, because it takes pounds | The shop must take them as if they were the same as pounds | The coins would turn into pounds at the till | The shop would give her double the value
test: Gran, a proper Kyiv nan, says "a hundred hryvnias is a fortune, sonechko" ("sonechko" is Ukrainian for "little sun", a pet name). How far does it go in Kyiv? | About 3 or 4 bags of crisps | Enough for a new bike | Nothing, hryvnias are not used in Kyiv | Exactly one bag of crisps
test: A video game says "Paris: everything costs 1 euro coin!" Your sis believes all French prices are tiny. What's wrong? | Prices in euros are normal prices, not tiny ones | Nothing, everything in France really costs one euro | Euros are worth ten pounds each, so it's dear | France uses pounds, not euros
test: Wei's mate goes "lowkey 50 yuan is loads, you're minted!" ("Lowkey" means quietly; "minted" means rich.) Is he? | No, 50 yuan is only a bit more than £5 | Yes, 50 yuan is worth about £500 in pounds | Yes, 50 yuan would buy a bike in London | No, yuan are worth nothing at all over here
test: Lucy thinks she'll pay exactly $4.99 from her UK account. What's more likely? | The price changes into pounds and may include a small fee | She will pay exactly 4.99 pounds and pence | The game will send the money back as dollars | The bank will pay it for her for free
test: Ivan asks why lots of European countries share the euro. What's one reason? | It makes trade and travel between them easier | It was the only kind of money left in the whole world | It means prices are the same in every single shop | It stops anyone from saving money for later on
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "currency"? | The particular form of money adopted by a given country | Any coin composed of a precious metal | The rate at which prices rise over time | A banknote valid in each and every country of the world
exam: It is given that an exchange bureau provides one euro and ten cents per pound sterling. Compute the quantity of euros obtained in exchange for one hundred pounds. | One hundred and ten euros | One hundred and twenty euros | One hundred euros | Ninety euros
exam: Identify the principal means whereby an exchange bureau derives its remuneration. | By furnishing customers a rate less favourable than the true rate | By charging customers in gold for each transaction | By printing additional foreign banknotes | By receiving payment from the airport authority alone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a price expressed as one hundred yen. | It constitutes a modest sum, being considerably less than one pound | It constitutes one hundred pounds sterling | It constitutes a sum in excess of fifty pounds | It is incapable of comparison with any other currency
